     <article class="story single" data-beat="space">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SPACE</span> · NASA · 2026-09-28 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Artemis Accords Surpasses 75 Signatories, Reaching 76 Nations</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Space</span> <span>#NASA</span> <span>#Artemis Accords</span> <span>#International Cooperation</span></div>
       <p>NASA recently marked a significant milestone for the Artemis Accords, celebrating the pact’s expansion to surpass 75 signatories. This growing international agreement, which outlines principles for peaceful and responsible space exploration, has now reached a total of 76 participating countries.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-09-28/artemis-accords-surpasses-75-signatories-reaching-76-nations.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The latest additions to the Accords include Albania, Croatia, Côte d’Ivoire, and San Marino. Their inclusion underscores a broadening global interest in collaborative space ventures and adherence to a common set of guiding principles for lunar and deep space activities. Each new signatory reinforces the international consensus on sustainable space practices.</p><p>A NASA statement highlighted the significance of this growth, noting, “Our momentum reflects a growing commitment to peaceful, responsible exploration and a shared understanding that the future in space will be shaped by those willing to lead.” This sentiment emphasizes the collaborative spirit intended by the Accords.</p><p>The Artemis Accords, led by the United States, establish a practical set of principles to guide cooperation among nations participating in NASA’s Artemis program, which aims to return humans to the Moon. The steady increase in signatories demonstrates a global alignment towards a shared vision for the future of human exploration beyond Earth orbit.</p><p>What to watch: Further expansion of the Artemis Accords and international space cooperation.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article correctly reports the expansion of the Artemis Accords based on the NASA source.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.nasa.gov/news-release/nasa-celebrates-as-artemis-accords-surpasses-75-signatories/" rel="noopener">NASA</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
