     <article class="story single" data-beat="business">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">BUSINESS</span> · BBC Business · 2026-09-28 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Best thing we can offer young people is a job, not benefits, says chancellor</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Business</span> <span>#Economy</span> <span>#Politics</span> <span>#Budget</span></div>
       <p>The chancellor has declared that the most beneficial provision for young people is a job, rather than benefits. This assertion was made during a speech delivered by John Healey.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-09-28/best-thing-we-can-offer-young-people-is-a-job-not-benefits-s.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The address comes at a pivotal moment, ahead of the government's next month's Budget. The chancellor is currently facing considerable pressure to bring down government borrowing costs, a key fiscal challenge that shapes current economic policy discussions. The need to reduce these costs is a significant factor influencing government spending and strategic priorities.</p><p>The remarks highlight a focus on employment as a primary mechanism for supporting the younger generation, framing it as a superior alternative to welfare provisions. This perspective is being advanced within a broader context of fiscal responsibility and efforts to stabilize the nation's finances. The upcoming Budget is expected to detail the specific policy directions and allocations that will reflect these priorities, as the administration continues to manage its economic agenda under scrutiny.</p><p>What to watch: The upcoming Budget statement will likely provide further details on government fiscal strategy.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Well-written elaboration of the source summary, maintaining factual alignment.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c3x2zw18ge59o?at_medium=RSS&amp;at_campaign=rss" rel="noopener">BBC Business</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
