     <article class="story single" data-beat="finance">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">FINANCE</span> · Livemint Money · 2026-09-28 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Disclosing foreign assets or income under FAST-DS 2026 is just the first step: Here's how to pay tax</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#personal finance</span> <span>#tax</span> <span>#foreign assets</span></div>
       <p>For ordinary savers and taxpayers, understanding recent financial disclosures is key to compliance. The FAST-DS 2026 scheme offers a pathway for eligible individuals to declare certain previously undisclosed foreign assets or income. This initiative aims to bring such holdings into the tax net, providing a structured mechanism for disclosure and allowing taxpayers to regularize their financial affairs.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-09-28/disclosing-foreign-assets-or-income-under-fast-ds-2026-is-ju.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>However, making a valid declaration under FAST-DS 2026 is merely the initial phase. Taxpayers who utilize this scheme must then adhere to a prescribed payment process to fulfill their obligations. This involves specific steps related to making the tax payment, often referencing forms like ITNS 289. It is crucial for those making disclosures to familiarize themselves with these procedures to ensure full compliance and avoid future complications. The scheme provides a framework for regularizing financial matters concerning foreign assets and income, emphasizing that disclosure must be followed by proper tax payment. For any individual who has previously held foreign assets or income without proper disclosure, and who meets the eligibility criteria for FAST-DS 2026, understanding this two-step process—disclosure and subsequent payment—is essential for peace of mind and legal adherence.</p><p>What to watch: The detailed steps for payment following a FAST-DS 2026 declaration.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Accurately reflects the source summary without adding unsupported details.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
