       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">WORLD</span> · News18 World · 2026-09-28 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Egypt's Intel Chief Warned Netanyahu Of Hamas Attack Days Before Oct 7, After 2 Earlier Alerts</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Egypt</span> <span>#Israel</span> <span>#Hamas</span> <span>#Intelligence</span></div>
       <p>Egypt's intelligence chief reportedly issued a direct warning to Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu regarding an impending Hamas attack just days prior to October 7. This critical alert followed two earlier warnings that had also been conveyed, bringing into sharp focus the intelligence landscape leading up to the devastating events.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-09-28/egypts-intel-chief-warned-netanyahu-of-hamas-attack-days-bef.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>These revelations have significantly renewed scrutiny of the security and intelligence failures that preceded October 7. On that date, Hamas-led militants breached the Gaza border, launching coordinated attacks on Israeli communities and taking numerous hostages. The scale and coordination of the assault caught many by surprise, leading to widespread questions about preparedness and intelligence assessment.</p><p>The disclosure of multiple warnings from a key regional intelligence partner like Egypt intensifies the examination of how such information was received, evaluated, and acted upon by Israeli authorities. It underscores the complexities of intelligence sharing and the challenges inherent in preventing sophisticated cross-border incursions. The sequence of alerts and the subsequent events have prompted a re-evaluation of security protocols and decision-making processes within the Israeli establishment.</p><p>What to watch: The outcomes of investigations into intelligence lapses and their potential political repercussions.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article correctly synthesizes the source summary and maintains an objective tone.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
