       <h1>Exclusive: No Su-57 Decision Yet, Says Defence Secretary. India Leans On AI Networks To Bridge Stealth Gaps - NDTV</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Defence</span> <span>#India</span> <span>#Su-57</span> <span>#AI</span></div>
       <p>The Defence Secretary has confirmed that no final decision has been reached concerning the acquisition of the Su-57 fighter jet, according to an exclusive report by NDTV. This statement provides clarity on the ongoing status of critical defence procurement programmes, indicating that assessments are still underway. Simultaneously, India is reportedly leaning on advanced Artificial Intelligence (AI) networks as a strategic measure to bridge existing stealth technology gaps within its military capabilities. This initiative underscores a proactive approach to modernising defence infrastructure through innovative technological integration. The deployment of AI is viewed as a vital component for enhancing operational effectiveness and situational awareness, particularly in scenarios where traditional stealth advantages may need augmentation. Defence officials have highlighted the increasing reliance on such indigenous technological solutions to maintain a competitive edge. The focus remains steadfast on developing self-reliant defence mechanisms and optimising the performance of current and future platforms. This dual strategy of careful procurement review and advanced technological adoption aims to ensure a robust and adaptive defence posture for the nation.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-09-28/exclusive-no-su-57-decision-yet-says-defence-secretary-india.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>What to watch: Further developments in India’s AI defence integration and the eventual decision regarding the Su-57 programme.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article accurately reflects the source summary and provides appropriate context.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
