       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">HEALTH</span> · ET Healthworld · 2026-09-28 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>India poised to globally lead next generation of medical practices: Jitendra Singh</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Health</span> <span>#India</span> <span>#Technology</span> <span>#Medicine</span></div>
       <p>India is poised to emerge as a global leader in the next generation of medical practices, according to the Minister of State in the Prime Minister's Office, Jitendra Singh. He described the current period as an exciting time for the field of medicine, driven by rapid technological advancements. The minister highlighted that the emergence of new technologies is actively opening up unprecedented possibilities within the healthcare sector, promising transformative changes in how medical care is delivered and experienced. However, Singh also underscored a significant challenge that lies ahead: achieving an optimal synergy between these technology-driven practices and traditional clinical medicine. The integration must be seamless and effective to fully harness the potential of innovation without compromising established medical principles. This balance is crucial for ensuring that technological progress translates into tangible benefits for patient care and public health, positioning India at the forefront of medical evolution.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-09-28/india-poised-to-globally-lead-next-generation-of-medical-pra.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>What to watch: Developments in policy and infrastructure supporting the integration of technology and clinical medicine in India.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article accurately expands on the source summary while maintaining factual consistency.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
