     <article class="story single" data-beat="environment">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">ENVIRONMENT</span> · Mongabay India · 2026-09-28 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Isolated island tribes should choose their own destinies, says Manish Chandi [Interview]</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Environment</span> <span>#India</span> <span>#North Sentinel Island</span> <span>#Indigenous Tribes</span></div>
       <p>North Sentinel Island, located within India’s Andaman and Nicobar archipelago, presents a unique case study in voluntary isolation and cultural preservation. From an aerial perspective, the tribal reserve appears as an unbroken expanse of dense, dark green foliage, sharply contrasting with the shimmering blue coastal waters that define its buffer zone. This visual distinction underscores the island's pristine and untouched nature.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-09-28/isolated-island-tribes-should-choose-their-own-destinies-say.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The island is home to the Sentinelese people, an indigenous community renowned for maintaining a lifestyle of complete self-sufficiency and minimal contact with the outside world. Their chosen isolation is rigorously safeguarded by a strict government no-contact policy, a measure designed to protect both the community and outsiders from potential harm and cultural disruption. This policy reflects a commitment to respecting their autonomy and way of life.</p><p>Within their verdant territory and surrounding waters, the Sentinelese sustain themselves through traditional practices such as spearing fish and hunting. These activities highlight their deep connection to their environment and their mastery of survival skills passed down through generations. The strict protective measures in place ensure that this ancient way of life can continue largely undisturbed, offering a rare glimpse into a society living entirely on its own terms, free from external influence. The island remains a testament to resilience and self-determination.</p><p>What to watch: Ongoing efforts to balance protection with the rights of isolated communities globally.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article accurately summarizes the provided source material while maintaining an appropriate tone.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://india.mongabay.com/2026/09/isolated-island-tribes-should-choose-their-own-destinies-says-manish-chandi-interview/" rel="noopener">Mongabay India</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
