     <article class="story single" data-beat="positive">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">POSITIVE</span> · The Better India · 2026-09-28 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Meet India’s Himalayan Grey Langur: The Leaf-Eating Primate of the Mountains</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Wildlife</span> <span>#Conservation</span> <span>#Himalaya</span> <span>#India</span></div>
       <p>The Himalayan grey langur, Semnopithecus schistaceus, is a captivating primate found across the majestic Himalayan range, including parts of northern India, Nepal, Bhutan, and Tibet. This leaf-eating species, often mistaken for the Kashmir grey langur, thrives at elevations from 1,500 to 4,000 meters, adapting its diet to the diverse mountain landscapes. Its distinctive features include a grey coat, dark face, long limbs, and a long tail, with males sometimes sporting a pronounced shoulder mane.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-09-28/meet-indias-himalayan-grey-langur-the-leaf-eating-primate-of.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Recent scientific advancements have helped clarify its classification. A 2020 study by researchers from the Indian Institute of Science and the Wildlife Institute of India, utilizing morphology, DNA, and ecology, confirmed Semnopithecus schistaceus as the species name for the Himalayan langur, distinguishing it from the plains Hanuman langur. This research is crucial for understanding the species.</p><p>Remarkably, these langurs are not confined to pristine forests but are also observed in human-dominated areas, coexisting with villages and agricultural fields. Their survival depends on the connectivity of their habitat, which spans national borders. Efforts to understand their distribution, like at Buxa Tiger Reserve, are vital. Preserving these connections across varied landscapes is paramount for their future.</p><p>Why it matters: Protecting the Himalayan grey langur ensures the biodiversity and ecological balance of its unique mountain habitats.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article provides a clear, accurate summary of the langur's taxonomy, habitat, and conservation challenges.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://thebetterindia.com/wildlife/himalayan-grey-langur-india-habitat-conservation-12575611" rel="noopener">The Better India</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
