     <article class="story single" data-beat="health">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">HEALTH</span> · ET Healthworld · 2026-09-28 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>SC Reserves Verdict on FSSAI's Front-of-Pack Food Labelling Rules</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#FSSAI</span> <span>#Food Labelling</span> <span>#Supreme Court</span> <span>#Public Health</span></div>
       <p>The Supreme Court has reserved its judgment concerning the proposed front-of-pack labelling framework for packaged food items. This significant regulatory initiative, spearheaded by the Food Safety and Standards Authority of India (FSSAI), aims to introduce clear, prominent warnings on products identified as high in sugar, fat, and salt. The primary objective is to enhance consumer awareness regarding the nutritional content of packaged foods, thereby enabling more informed dietary choices.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-09-28/sc-reserves-verdict-on-fssais-front-of-pack-food-labelling-r.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>During the recent hearing, a counsel representing a non-governmental organization (NGO) presented a key suggestion. The NGO's argument emphasized that the thresholds used for these warnings should consider the total sugar and total fat content present in products, rather than limiting the scope to only added components. This approach advocates for a more holistic and comprehensive assessment of a food item's nutritional profile.</p><p>In anticipation of its final decision, the court has directed all parties involved in the proceedings to submit their concluding written arguments within a three-day timeframe. This directive marks a critical phase in the legal process, indicating that the court is moving towards a definitive ruling on these important public health regulations. The outcome is keenly awaited by both public health advocates and the food manufacturing industry.</p><p>What to watch: The Supreme Court's final verdict and its implications for consumer health and food industry standards.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Accurately reflects source; no unsupported claims.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://health.economictimes.indiatimes.com/news/policy/sc-reserves-verdict-on-fssais-front-of-pack-food-labelling-rules-seeks-final-submissions/134538498" rel="noopener">ET Healthworld</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
