     <article class="story single" data-beat="environment">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">ENVIRONMENT</span> · Guardian Environment · 2026-09-28 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Trump administration to slash clean car rules aimed at cutting climate emissions</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Environment</span> <span>#Regulations</span> <span>#Automotive</span> <span>#Climate Change</span></div>
       <p>The Trump administration announced on Monday its decision to significantly reduce existing clean car regulations, a move that has drawn strong criticism from environmental groups. These rules were originally established with the dual purpose of curtailing planet-heating emissions and encouraging the development and adoption of electric vehicles within the automotive sector.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-09-28/trump-administration-to-slash-clean-car-rules-aimed-at-cutti.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The new policy changes are set to relax specific requirements previously placed on automakers. These requirements had mandated controls on pollution originating from gasoline-powered cars and light trucks. The scope of this regulatory shift will apply to vehicle models produced for the years 2022 through 2031. Environmentalists have voiced their concern regarding the administration's action, framing it as a setback for efforts to combat climate change and improve air quality. The original clean car standards were a key component of national environmental policy, designed to drive technological innovation towards cleaner transportation options and reduce the overall carbon footprint of the vehicle fleet. The administration's stated rationale for the changes has not been provided in the summary, but the impact is clear: a loosening of environmental controls on future vehicle production.</p><p>What to watch: The long-term environmental and economic implications of these revised standards.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article faithfully reflects the provided summary regarding the clean car regulations.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/sep/28/trump-administration-slashes-clean-car-rules" rel="noopener">Guardian Environment</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
