     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-09-28 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Worm Experiments Reveal How Epigenetic Effects Persist Across Generations</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Science</span> <span>#Genetics</span> <span>#Epigenetics</span> <span>#Research</span></div>
       <p>Every cell within an organism contains the same complete DNA, which holds all the instructions necessary for its function. Despite this fundamental genetic uniformity, not all cells activate the identical set of genes. A nerve cell, for instance, does not utilize the same genes as a muscle cell, a distinction crucial for their specialized roles and the overall complexity of the organism.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-09-28/worm-experiments-reveal-how-epigenetic-effects-persist-acros.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>This precise control over gene activity, occurring without altering the underlying DNA sequence, is defined as epigenetics. Epigenetic mechanisms are sophisticated systems that regulate when and where genes are expressed. They dictate which genes are turned on or off in specific cell types, thereby allowing for the development and maintenance of diverse tissues and organs from a single genetic blueprint. These processes are essential for cellular differentiation, enabling cells to adopt specialized functions despite sharing identical genetic information.</p><p>Insights into these critical biological processes have recently emerged from worm experiments. These studies contribute to a deeper understanding of how gene expression can be modulated, influencing cellular identity and function in a dynamic manner. The research highlights the significance of these non-genetic factors in biological regulation, providing a clearer picture of how gene activity is managed.</p><p>What to watch: Further research is expected to clarify the specific mechanisms of epigenetic regulation.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article accurately and smoothly expands on the source summary without adding false claims.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-09-worm-reveal-epigenetic-effects-persist.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
       <div class="sharebar" data-href="/article/2026-09-28/worm-experiments-reveal-how-epigenetic-effects-persist-acros" data-title="Worm Experiments Reveal How Epigenetic Effects Persist Across Generations"><b>Share:</b>
